    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. You mentioned \"friction events\" in relation to roundabouts. Could you expand on what specifically causes those friction points in a roundabout system compared to a signalized one? I'm looking to understand the mechanical or behavioral causes for these interruptions in flow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you refer to friction points, you are likely discussing the mechanical conflict points and vehicle-to-vehicle interactions inherent in roundabout geometry. Roundabouts create inherent yield requirements, meaning drivers must continuously check for traffic in all directions, which increases driver reaction times and stabilizes flow through smaller, more frequent merging actions. In contrast, signalized intersections mechanically shut down specific arms entirely, creating distinct start-up waves of traffic that move in platoons rather than a continuous stream, which reduces friction between individual vehicles but creates stop-and-go friction at the signal line itself. Situations like spiral roundabouts or large diameter circles can further disrupt driver anticipation and merging dynamics, often leading to unexpected stops and lane changes that interrupt flow, whereas signals provide predictable stop times. For high-volume operations, this behavioral difference means roundabouts rely on smooth engagement to maintain speed, while signals excel at forcing orderly queue progression despite the stops.",
      "source": "target",
